From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: pass the colour aspects and HDR10 metadata of
 10-bit streams

The encoder had no colour aspects or HDR metadata parameters. A Main 10
stream therefore carried no colour description: a BT.2020 PQ (HDR10) or
BT.2020 SDR stream was decoded as BT.709 wherever the container has no
colour box (a raw .hevc file, a player that goes by the bitstream). And
the framework lists the HEVC Main 10 HDR10 profile only for encoders
that declare C2StreamHdrStaticInfo on their input, so apps could not ask
for HDR10, and no mastering display or content light level SEI was
written.

With a 10-bit profile the interface now declares, as the software
encoders do, the colour aspects of the input (C2StreamColorAspectsInfo
::input) and the ones the stream carries (::output, the VUI aspects).
When the device also has the V4L2 HDR10 controls
(V4L2_CID_COLORIMETRY_HDR10_CLL_INFO and _MASTERING_DISPLAY, looked up
with VIDIOC_QUERY_EXT_CTRL), it declares C2StreamHdrStaticInfo::input,
and the framework lists Main 10 HDR10. Without the controls no HDR
profile is listed: HDR10 streams without the metadata would fail clients
that check for it.

A 10-bit session passes the colour aspects to the device as the
colorimetry of the raw format (colour space for the primaries, transfer
function, Y'CbCr encoding, quantization range), when that colorimetry
describes exactly them. A driver completes a field left at DEFAULT from
the colour space, as V4L2 defines a default transfer function and
encoding for each, so primaries without a transfer function or a matrix
would put a value nobody chose into the stream: a BT.2020 colour space
with an unspecified transfer function gets the BT.709 one. Such aspects,
and aspects that V4L2 has no value for (the HLG transfer function, P3
primaries with a D65 white point, gamma 2.2, other ranges), leave the
whole colorimetry to the driver, and the stream has no colour
description; players then use the container's, where the framework
records the aspects. Aspects without primaries cannot be described
either: the V4L2 core resets the transfer function, the encoding and the
quantization when the colour space is DEFAULT. An unspecified range next
to other aspects goes to the device as limited range: the VUI carries
the range flag with any colour description, and limited is what video is
unless said otherwise, and what a decoder infers without the flag. The
conversion from C2 colour aspects to V4L2 colorimetry lives in
EncodeHelpers, next to the level conversions, as the interface needs it
too. The HDR10 static metadata goes to the two controls before
streaming; values outside the control ranges (a minimum luminance of 0)
are clamped, and a device that refuses a control gets a warning, not an
error, as the metadata is optional. Without static metadata from the
client the controls are not set; a device may still describe a PQ stream
with metadata of its own (the iris driver with the HDR10 controls writes
a default mastering display, as venus does).

The VUI aspects (C2StreamColorAspectsInfo::output) report what the
stream carries. Until the encoder starts they follow the configured
aspects: for Main 10 the input aspects when they become colorimetry as
above, with an unspecified range as limited, and all unspecified
otherwise and for Main. When the encoder starts, the component sets
them, under the interface lock, to the description it gave the device,
and they keep it until the encoder stops: the description is part of the
raw format, which cannot change once the encoder has allocated its
buffers, so colour aspects configured meanwhile apply from the next
start.

RGB frames in a 10-bit session are converted as in an 8-bit session,
with the BT.601 matrix into limited range, whatever the configured
aspects say, and a stream that claimed those aspects (BT.2020, say)
would be decoded with the wrong matrix. When the first input frame is
RGB, the component therefore starts the encoder again, before it has
seen a frame, with the colour description of the conversion: the BT.601
matrix and limited range, with the BT.709 primaries and the sRGB
transfer function: the RGB blocks do not carry theirs, 8-bit RGB buffers
are sRGB unless said otherwise, and V4L2 describes a matrix only next to
primaries; and without HDR10 metadata, which describes PQ content. The
VUI aspects report the same. The description goes into the raw format,
which cannot change once the encoder has allocated its buffers, hence
the restart. If the encoder cannot be started again, the component
reports the error once and returns that work, and the work queued until
the client stops it, unprocessed, as flushed work.

The container of such a stream keeps the configured aspects. RGB frames
reach the encoder through input surfaces only (CCodec has no byte-buffer
image for the RGB layout of an RGBA_8888 block), and with an input
surface the framework writes the colour of the container from the
surface's dataspace, which follows the configured aspects unless the
producer sets another; no parameter of the component changes it. A
stream configured as BT.2020 thus has a BT.2020 container around a
BT.601 description, and for the two to agree the conversion would have
to use the matrix and range of the dataspace.

8-bit sessions pass neither, as before: their usual sources, camera and
screen recording, have not been checked against the colour aspects the
framework attaches to them, and RGB input is converted with a fixed
BT.601 matrix whatever the aspects say, so writing the aspects into
their streams could mislabel recordings that play correctly today. The
AVC encoder and HEVC on a device without 10-bit input declare none of
the new parameters.

